  "query_preview": "..Latest visible satellite imagery shows mostly sunny \nskies from Tucson westward and partly sunny skies east of Tucson \nwhere cumulus clouds are starting to build up in the past hour or \ntwo. We're not seeing convection there quiet yet, but given adequate \nmoisture (PWAT around 1 inch) and MLCAPE values 500-1000 J/KG per \nlatest RAP mesoanalysis, that will be enough to result in isolated \nto widely scattered showers and thunderstorms this afternoon into \nearly evening, primarily across Cochise County but also extending \nnorthward into Graham and Greenlee County as well. The main concern \nwill continue to be gusty outflow winds over 40 mph that can produce \nblowing dust. Can't rule out a couple of back-building/training \nstorms with isolated heavy rain threat given the southwest flow \npattern with modest 15-20 kts of 0-6km bulk shear under the \nsouthwest flow aloft. Locales from Tucson westward will stay dry as \ndrier air starts to move in from the west ahead of an approaching \ntrough over the west coast.\n\nThe troughing across the western US will be the dominant player in \nour weather over the weekend into early next week. The southwest \nflow will help scour out residual moisture this weekend, although \njust enough moisture is expected to remain Saturday for a slight \nchance (10 to 20 percent) of showers and thunderstorms in far SE \nCochise County. Dry conditions will prevail elsewhere with breezy to \nlocally windy conditions under southwest flow aloft both Saturday \nand Sunday with critical fire weather conditions resulting in a Red \nFlag Warning for most of southeast Arizona.\n\nAs troughiness dominates through most of next week that will result \nin a continuation of dry conditions for southeast Arizona. Still \nelevated to locally near critical fire weather conditions Monday \nwith afternoon breezes each day. We're going to have to wait till \nthe end of next week at the earliest for glimpses of better monsoon \nmoisture, and even that looks mostly limited to locales near the \nAZ/NM border as the flow becomes more southerly. Better hints of a \nmore favorable Four Corners high building around or just after July \n4th which would bring a more favorable monsoonal flow pattern into \nthe area. \n\nHigh temperatures will remain above normal through Saturday dropping \nto near or slightly below normal Sunday through Wednesday, then near \nnormal on Thursday.  With lower dewpoints early next week, that will \nalso result in more noticeably cool mornings with lows into the \nupper 60s to around 70 for Tucson.",
